WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.980
A couple of years ago,

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.193
I went to a totally normal party.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:09.090
It was put on by the well-muscled

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.193
brain explorer, Bryan Johnson.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.083
He held the event at this
house in Venice, California.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.743
Billionaire Patrick Collison
was there and so was his smile.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.190
The guests all got together,
donned Bluetooth headphones

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:27.640
and listened to music written
by an AI and performed

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.463
by "American Idol"
contestant Taryn Southern.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.700
Oh, and you can also take a whiff

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.630
of an extinct Hawaiian
plant because some people

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.990
who play with DNA had found a way

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:41.483
to bring its smell back to life.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.860
The folks who did the DNA manipulation

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.260
work at Ginkgo Bioworks.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.410
The company is perhaps the world leader

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.623
in the field of synthetic biology.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:02.800
Earlier this year, Ginkgo
raised a staggering $2.5 billion

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.563
on its way to being valued at 15 billion.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:12.610
To find out what $2.5
billion buys you these days,

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:14.290
I went to Ginkgo's headquarters

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:18.720
in Boston, Massachusetts

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.710
for it is here

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.850
or make that here

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:29.703
that the company is building
out a DNA superhighway.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.330
Ginkgo was founded back in
2009 by several big brains

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:54.523
from MIT, including its CEO Jason Kelly.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.660
The founders wanted to develop ways

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.230
to treat DNA more or
less like software code.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.100
You find an organism with
an interesting property,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.250
snip out the DNA behind that property

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.683
and then shove the DNA
fragment into something else.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:16.270
The big promise behind synthetic
biology is that we'll find

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.683
a new means to harness nature
and bend it to our will.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:23.790
We'll be able to manufacture
things like chemicals

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:28.163
and fuels and even physical
objects in ultra efficient ways.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.940
To be clear, we're also talking
about eventually getting

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.740
into Jurassic Park mad
scientist-style stuff

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.040
and this grand experiment could end up

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.490
with you being a reptile's breakfast.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:44.340
The core idea behind the company
was inside of every cell,

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.140
every bacteria, plant,
animal on planet earth

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.899
is digital code in the form of DNA.

00:02:49.899 --> 00:02:53.410
So it's A, T, Cs and Gs, not
zeroes and ones like you'd see

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.980
in a computer, but you can read that code

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.940
with DNA sequencing and
importantly, you can write that code

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:01.140
with DNA printing.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.250
And this is for a bio nerd
like me, this is magic.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.070
You literally go on a
computer, you type ATCGGGG,

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:09.080
you hit print and out of
machines in this facility

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.690
or the ones next door comes a piece of DNA

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:13.420
that's exactly what you ordered.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.480
And then you can put that into a cell.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.160
And our idea was if you can
read and write code and you have

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.500
a machine that'll run it, which
is how we thought of a cell,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.530
you're programming just
like you program a computer.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.440
And so Ginkgo's business is
basically programmer tools

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.363
so that other folks can
develop new programs for cells.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.770
This core synthetic biology principle

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.723
of treating DNA like
code is simple in theory.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.020
In most cases, scientists
will grab the DNA they want

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:48.700
and insert it into something
like a bacteria or yeast.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.320
Then they create lots of
copies of the organisms

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.820
in much the same way as we make beer.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.750
They feed the organisms
sugar and nutrients

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:58.590
and grow them in a vat.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.050
So I'll give you an example.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:03.230
There was a flower that went extinct

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:06.850
in I think it was the
early 1900s in Hawaii,

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:09.020
but before it went extinct,
it had gotten collected

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:10.980
by scientists and then stored

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.590
and it's actually it was at
the Harvard Botanical Museum.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.270
And so we collected some of that sample,

00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:17.750
brought it back here to Gingko,

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.240
put it through our DNA sequencer.

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:22.670
So we read the DNA code
of that extinct flower.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:26.890
We found the part of the DNA
that encoded for the scent.

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.440
So we'd take those 6,000 letters of DNA

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:33.480
and move them into a
bacterial genome like E. coli.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:34.490
So how do you do that?

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.130
Well, you have a special enzyme

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.800
that basically can find
a location on the genome

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.770
that looks for ATGGs, whatever one is

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:43.220
on the specific code it has and it cuts.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:46.980
And then it opens and then
you add in those 6,000 letters

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.740
from the extinct flower genome.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:49.730
How did you get them?

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:52.090
Well, you read them, you
had them on the computer,

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:54.410
you put them in ATCG, hit print

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.580
and you printed a piece of synthetic DNA.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.700
So now you add that in to where you cut

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.810
and then you patch it up a
little bit, grow the cell.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.210
Instead of just doing the normal
swim around bacteria thing,

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.580
it also now makes this fragrance.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.030
And so we actually resurrected the scent

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.340
of this extinct flower as
a project that ended up

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.100
it's actually showing
at a few art museums.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.733
To kind of bring it back.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:21.200
To see some of this process in action,

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.023
I went to visit Sara in
one of Ginkgo's labs.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.770
Okay, so somebody, you've made a microbe,

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:30.390
you've engineered it with
a specific bit of DNA

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:32.680
to give it a quality
that you are looking for

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:34.790
and then you guys are both making those

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:37.420
and checking that
they're coming out right.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:40.000
We're testing a lot of
different engineered strains

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.430
to help us build our
code base to understand

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.020
which of those pieces of DNA are able

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:48.740
to achieve the desired outcome.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:52.800
Okay, and so you've got
these cells over here.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.967
Some of them have stuff on them, the white

00:05:54.967 --> 00:05:56.040
and some of them don't.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.770
So I assume the ones with the
white is ones that have worked

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.250
and that's what you're looking for.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:02.490
Yeah, so each of those individual segments

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:05.050
is a particular engineered
strain that we are trying

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:08.160
to isolate in order to
then test downstream.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.720
The segments that have the
individual white circles

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:15.650
which we call colonies have
successfully taken up that DNA.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:17.230
So this would be the beginning stage

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:21.180
of you'd get what you want
and then you can grow it

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.360
in bigger quantities.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:23.330
You can study it, yeah.

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:26.563
So this is the very start
of that kind of pipeline.

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:33.870
What Gingko really wants
to do is automate all

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:38.263
of this and make meddling
with DNA fast and cheap.

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:44.803
To do that, you need a factory.

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:48.880
This is perhaps the most
modern DNA assembly line

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:50.270
on the planet.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:53.490
Molecules go in at one
end of the conveyor belt

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:56.740
and robots take care of
all the processes needed

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:58.713
to refine them into something useful.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.640
So this is a magnetic propulsion system

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:09.090
that has individual carts.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:10.630
And the robots

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:13.040
or the humans will load
samples onto these carts

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:14.850
and then the system will know

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.970
which destination they need to take them.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:19.460
So a human comes here and puts a sample,

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:20.700
feeds it into the system.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.260
Exactly, there you go.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:25.950
And now you can press
the button over here.

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:29.000
And then off it goes.

00:07:35.497 --> 00:07:37.182
And the green robot is gonna grab them.

00:07:37.182 --> 00:07:38.940
Now, you can see the
green robots pick it up

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.920
and will place it on the
second station there.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:45.340
And this is when the yellow
robot will come into play.

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.800
Your little yellow robot will
remove some of the samples,

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:49.573
add controls in their place.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.630
The tube rack then goes to
a next oscillating station,

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:57.150
which is a centrifuge work cell here.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:59.820
Spin it around, so essentially
to try to remove bubbles

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:01.280
or anything that might have formed

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:02.679
during shipping of the samples.

00:08:02.679 --> 00:08:05.240
Okay, I think our sample's off again.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:07.500
So now we're sealing the plates

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:10.025
and it can go over the
rest of the process.

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:16.993
It all ends

00:08:16.993 --> 00:08:20.270
with an incredibly
sophisticated trash can.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:22.610
These specialized trash work cells

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:25.520
have these specialized
carts that weigh the amount

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:27.380
of trash that there is
in each of these bags

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:28.820
to know when they are full.

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:32.130
And finally, the robots can
actually place the trash

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:34.530
at random positions around the bag

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:37.120
to make sure they're
not always stacking up

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:39.460
in the center making very large towers

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:41.470
that can eventually break the system.

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:44.700
And does a human have to
take this out at the end?

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:46.240
This is the last step

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:47.794
where the human is still in the loop.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:52.773
Step forward, one helpful TV host.

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:05.830
I remember around 2008, it
was getting easier a bit

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:08.650
to manipulate DNA and we could
see where things were going,

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:12.300
but people were already saying
in a few years we're gonna be

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.360
you will program a cell to make a chair

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:16.490
and we won't have chair factories anymore,

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:17.323
we'll just grow them.

00:09:17.323 --> 00:09:21.360
And so how much of that
vision do you still believe in

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:22.970
and how close are we to this idea

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:26.150
that we're gonna just be making

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:29.990
fantastic new things out of biology?

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:31.208
I think it's all coming, yeah.

00:09:31.208 --> 00:09:32.041
You do?

00:09:32.041 --> 00:09:34.610
Yeah, the reason is I can't
emphasize this enough,

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:37.400
people take biology massively for granted.

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.970
Every other engineering
field was designed by humans

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:41.390
from the bottom up.

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:44.140
We know every little
bolt in that Ford car,

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:48.483
every little gate in that
semiconductor chip from Intel.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:54.280
Well, biology is ridiculously advanced

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.480
and we're just entering
the era of being able

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:58.120
to program it to do new things.

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.558
We know it's capable of big things.

00:10:00.558 --> 00:10:02.830
The only limit is human
understanding to be able

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:05.393
to program it, but we know if we learn,

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:08.740
it can do this stuff because
it's already doing it.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:10.470
It's making our food,
it's cleaning our water,

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:12.020
it's making the air.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:13.400
We're made out of it.

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:17.400
And the one magical feature
of it and it is a miracle

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:20.790
is it's programmable because
inside of it is digital code

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:23.033
that we can as humans read and write.

00:10:24.715 --> 00:10:27.203
Ginkgo does go after some weird stuff.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.880
This dog, for example used
to be a tea cup poodle

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:34.993
just last week.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:39.370
And clearly, people here really do dream

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:42.343
of doing things like
resurrecting the dinosaurs.

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:47.160
But as with any new technology,

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:50.890
I wanted to know how synthetic
biology would benefit me,

00:10:50.890 --> 00:10:52.253
like right now.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:59.550
And so I took what we'll call
the blood elevator express

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:03.653
a couple floors down to another
startup, Motif Foodworks.

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:08.290
Motif is a research and
development company for food

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:13.083
that uses synthetic biology
to make food taste better.

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:18.793
Today, I'm here to eat a burger,
only no cow was involved.

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:22.430
Motif teamed with Ginkgo
to take the DNA code

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:25.380
for hemoglobin, plop it into a yeast

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:29.693
and then grow what amounts
to a vat of faux blood.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:34.313
This fellow here is adding
the blood to some soy,

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:37.173
throwing in some seasonings.

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:45.723
And then, voila.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:51.453
Thank you so much.

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:53.820
Well, should we try it
while it's still hot?

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:55.370
Absolutely, that's the best way.

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:56.203
Okay.

00:12:01.646 --> 00:12:02.479
It's good.

00:12:02.479 --> 00:12:07.479
It's burgery, for sure and
it has that on the char

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:08.403
and all that.

00:12:08.403 --> 00:12:10.650
The char, the bite quality is much better.

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:14.520
I would say that old
generations of plant-based meat,

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:17.150
the first bite, you might
get this crust on the outside

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:18.050
like you see here,

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:21.000
but the second bite would be more mushy.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:22.840
The inside would not have texture.

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:24.600
So look at all the effort.

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:26.740
I mean, that pretty much looks like

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:28.100
what you might see in a burger.

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:31.000
You notice all the pieces,
the way it crumbles.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:32.470
The other thing I noticed is

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:35.720
there's definitely that
umami kind of note on there.

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:40.090
Well, our ingredient we call
hemami and it's essentially

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:44.820
the molecule in the muscle
of a cow that makes beef red,

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:49.280
it makes it give you that
beautiful serumy, bloody flavor.

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:52.290
It catalyzes a whole bunch
of the chemistry in there.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:55.340
And that is an amazing piece of technology

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:58.483
that Ginkgo Bioworks created for us.

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:06.900
Ginkggo was able to look at
300 different muscle proteins

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:09.850
from different species, mini sperm whales,

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:11.000
one that comes to mind,

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:16.870
tuna, pork, chicken, a whole
series of different birds.

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:17.913
And they were already sequenced,

00:13:17.913 --> 00:13:20.560
so it wasn't like we
took any animal material.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:23.910
When they did the sequences, it was for--

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:27.680
Specific muscle protein,
a class called myoglobins.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:30.590
Ginkgo got us the ability to look

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:32.160
at all the different properties,

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:34.650
stability, taste, flavor, color.

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:37.410
And then once we honed
in on the ones we wanted,

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:39.560
we asked them to optimize the yeast,

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:42.850
to turn that into a mini
factory so that we can make it

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:46.980
and so we can have the great taste of beef

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:48.280
without using any animals.

00:13:57.100 --> 00:13:58.960
To put a fine symbolic point

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:02.510
on how all of this stuff gets
made, I felt it essential

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:04.963
to go to a nearby brewery with Jason.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:13.620
I thought being in a brewery was--

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:14.833
Yeah, ideal, right?

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:21.840
They use yeast, but no
commercial beer today is made

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:23.393
with engineered cells.

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:27.490
Have you ever had a non-alcoholic
beer, it tastes like shit?

00:14:27.490 --> 00:14:29.980
Well, that's because it's
getting rid of the alcohol,

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:32.780
you're pulling off all
the flavor components.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:34.370
And so could you make a yeast

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:35.580
that doesn't produce much alcohol

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:36.940
that gives you all the taste?

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:38.560
So you end up with an alcohol-free beer

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:39.860
that actually tastes good.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.387
Brewing is an industry that
has biology so intrinsic to it

00:14:43.387 --> 00:14:45.770
that's actually an
opportunity to figure that out

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:47.340
and do it correctly.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:48.200
We've worked on the breweries.

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:51.190
I think it's gonna come
around pretty soon actually.

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:52.810
Thank you.
Oh, look at that.

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:55.330
Thanks, cheers, man, to yeast.

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:56.180
Yeah, to yeast.

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:01.750
Oh, that's good.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:03.680
I wanted to go back just a little bit.

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:04.513
Where'd you grow up?

00:15:04.513 --> 00:15:05.346
I grew up in Florida.

00:15:05.346 --> 00:15:06.770
Yeah, yeah, South Florida.

00:15:06.770 --> 00:15:09.063
And what was your family a family of?

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:11.940
We weren't a family of PhD,

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:13.753
I was the first person to get a PhD.

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:17.380
But were you even good in
high school and everything,

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:18.720
was science your thing?

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:25.010
Yeah, honestly I think what
it was I was 12, 13 years old

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:26.540
when Jurassic Park came out.

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:31.180
And I was a big sci-fi
nerd, I was reading books.

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:34.180
I read all those Crichton
books, I love that stuff.

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:37.203
And I think I fell in love with it then.

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:46.283
What kid wouldn't dream of playing God?

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:50.810
To that end, I wanted to know
when we'd get past perfumes

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:54.490
and fast food blood and
get to the good stuff,

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:55.693
the freaky stuff.

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:59.940
Well, I was talking with the
guys that help make the show.

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:01.410
This is one of the lingering questions

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:02.930
that we still weren't sure about.

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:05.270
I can get the enzymes,
they'd cut and paste DNA.

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:09.120
I can get it to make the myoglobin,

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:11.960
but then why can't you grow an arm

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:14.160
is what we kept talking about in the car.

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:16.330
It's like why can't you put
in the code for the arm then?

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:17.976
If you can put in the
code for the protein,

00:16:17.976 --> 00:16:19.680
why can't you do the whole thing?

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:22.150
It's a great question and this is like me

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:23.510
when I got to MIT.

00:16:23.510 --> 00:16:26.103
I'm like, I thought I would
be making arms or something.

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:32.010
The short answer is, we don't understand

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:37.010
the specific sequence of
DNA, how it affects the cell.

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:41.000
It's just because we didn't design cells

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.360
and they're complicated.

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:46.040
And so if up put in some
new DNA that was like,

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:49.260
I think this DNA will make
an arm, it just wouldn't.

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:52.680
But it might make some
other deranged thing.

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:54.630
But it's like salamanders.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:57.560
The frickin salamander
grows arms but humans don't.

00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:01.190
And so it's just a programming problem

00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:02.110
between here and there,

00:17:02.110 --> 00:17:05.200
but we know the substrate
is capable of it.

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:06.427
Obviously, everyone thinks
about climate change

00:17:06.427 --> 00:17:07.260
all the time.

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:10.306
And I don't wanna give people false hope

00:17:10.306 --> 00:17:12.390
on any of this stuff, but
something that's just gobble it.

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:15.580
Why can't we just gobble
carbon and shit out happiness

00:17:15.580 --> 00:17:16.940
on the other side?

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:17.940
Where is that?

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:20.485
I'll give you a real one,
I'll give you a real project.

00:17:20.485 --> 00:17:22.350
So the project we're doing,

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:25.850
we have $100 million joint
venture with Bayer CropScience.

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:27.670
You know synthetic nitrogen fertilizer?

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:30.950
Okay, the way you make it,
you basically pull nitrogen

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:32.040
out of the air.

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:33.360
You're basically gonna take that nitrogen,

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:35.890
you're gonna combine it with
hydrogen to make ammonia,

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:37.600
which is fertilizer.

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:38.880
Where do you get the hydrogen?

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.620
Oh, burning natural gas.

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:42.366
Okay, so you burn natural gas.

00:17:42.366 --> 00:17:44.780
I think it's like 4% global
greenhouse gas is going

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:48.470
to frickin fertilizer,
but hey, we get to eat.

00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:49.730
We need fertilizer.

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:53.230
Okay, well, there are
certain crops like soybeans

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:56.030
that don't need much fertilizer, why?

00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:58.000
They have microbes in their roots,

00:17:58.000 --> 00:17:59.490
they're literally pulling
nitrogen out of the air--

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:01.020
Nature doing all the work.

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:05.700
It's like you plant a legume
and it refertilizes the soil.

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:08.490
So we can go find the code
that produces fertilizer

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:12.660
for crops in soybeans,
we can take microbes

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:17.160
that are friendly with corn,
install that code and then

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:19.340
a business that Bayer already
has you do a seed treatment.

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:22.310
You basically coat the
seed in those bacteria

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:24.530
and when you plant it,
they grow along with it

00:18:24.530 --> 00:18:26.380
and they produce fertilizer for the crop.

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:27.213
That's the project.

00:18:27.213 --> 00:18:29.720
So that's one where if
you actually succeeded,

00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:31.340
not only is it a $70 billion market,

00:18:31.340 --> 00:18:33.780
it's a 4% global greenhouse gas reduction.

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:35.360
No, that's cool.

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:38.100
But no, nothing's just out there

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:40.130
gobbling CO2 out of the air yet.

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:41.110
Well, trees.

00:18:41.110 --> 00:18:42.744
Yeah.

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:46.350
That's an amazing, amazing machine.

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:48.710
And it is like when you fly in an airplane

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:50.720
and look out the window, what do you see?

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:54.240
Trees, that is real scale.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:57.930
None of this crap like our
auto plants and chip fabs,

00:18:57.930 --> 00:18:59.340
it's a joke.

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:00.590
The only real scale on the planet

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:02.834
that's actually making
anything is biology.

00:19:02.834 --> 00:19:04.070
The only thing we're good at making is CO2

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:05.170
and fucking things up.

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:18.670
To Jason's point, humans
do have a tendency

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:21.033
to run amok when given new toys.

00:19:21.890 --> 00:19:25.053
And this could all go
bad, Jurassic Park bad.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:30.000
That said, synthetic biology
is the area of technology

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.260
that excites me the most.

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:35.290
The stuff bubbling away
in these jars and flowing

00:19:35.290 --> 00:19:38.110
through these tubes
represents our best shot

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:40.770
at fixing many of the messes we've made

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:43.883
and at doing so many other
things better in the future.

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:47.850
It's certainly not communing with nature

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:50.530
in any traditional sense, but it's an area

00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:53.730
where humans can meld
science and creativity

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:56.003
in previously unimaginable ways.

00:19:57.860 --> 00:19:59.753
In DNA, there is hope.

